From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: James Gardner <jamestexasgardner@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] init: mount a bounded tmpfs on /tmp

Init mounted /dev, /proc and /sys, and touched /tmp only in TDX builds,
so a guest whose rootfs carries no tmp/ entry had no /tmp at all. A
rootfs imported from an OCI image never does: its manifest lists files,
so an image's empty tmp/ does not survive. Programs that assume /tmp,
such as Dolt's sql-server, then fail with "open /tmp: no such file or
directory".

On every non-TDX boot, init now mounts a tmpfs on /tmp before the
workload starts. It is mode 1777, nosuid and nodev, and is sized at 25%
of the guest's RAM, so a write past that bound fails with ENOSPC and
/tmp cannot take most of the memory the workload was granted. Init
creates the mount point if the rootfs lacks it, and mounts only over a
real directory: mount(2) follows a symbolic link, so a /tmp that is a
link or a file stops the guest instead. The tmpfs is
memory-backed and lasts one boot, so nothing written there reaches the
host or outlives the guest. A mount that fails stops the guest before
the workload runs.

Signed-off-by: James Gardner <jamestexasgardner@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/src/init_blob/init/init.c b/src/init_blob/init/init.c
index 1f4b89d..753916a 100644
--- a/src/init_blob/init/init.c
+++ b/src/init_blob/init/init.c
@@ -542,6 +542,58 @@ static int chroot_luks()
 }
 #endif
 
+#if __linux__ && !defined(TDX)
+/*
+ * A writable /tmp for the workload, which POSIX programs assume and which a
+ * rootfs need not carry (an image's empty tmp/ holds no file, so a rootfs
+ * built from it has none): a tmpfs, mode 1777 like any /tmp, nosuid and
+ * nodev, mounted before the workload starts. It is memory-backed and
+ * per-boot, so nothing written there outlives the guest or reaches the
+ * host, and it adds no reach. Whatever the rootfs holds under tmp/ is
+ * hidden beneath it.
+ *
+ * Its size is bounded at TMP_SIZE_PERCENT of the guest's RAM. tmpfs pages
+ * count against the guest's memory, which the hypervisor bounds; the
+ * kernel's default of half of RAM would let /tmp alone take half of what
+ * the workload was granted. A quarter gives a database or a build room for
+ * its scratch files. A write past the bound fails with ENOSPC. /dev/shm
+ * keeps the kernel default, so the two together can pin three quarters of
+ * the guest's RAM; either way the guest cannot exceed what its grant sized
+ * the VM at, and the bound is a usability limit inside the guest, which
+ * the workload can remount, not a security boundary.
+ *
+ * TDX builds use /tmp for their root volume and keep upstream's handling.
+ */
+#define TMP_SIZE_PERCENT 25
+
+static int mount_tmp(void)
+{
+    char options[32];
+    struct stat existing;
+
+    if (mkdir("/tmp", 01777) < 0 && errno != EEXIST) {
+        perror("mkdir(/tmp)");
+        return -1;
+    }
+    /*
+     * mount(2) follows a symbolic link in its target, so a rootfs whose
+     * tmp is a link would get the tmpfs wherever the link points. Only a
+     * real directory is mounted over; anything else stops the guest.
+     */
+    if (lstat("/tmp", &existing) < 0 || !S_ISDIR(existing.st_mode)) {
+        printf("/tmp is not a directory, refusing to mount over it\n");
+        return -1;
+    }
+    snprintf(options, sizeof(options), "mode=1777,size=%d%%", TMP_SIZE_PERCENT);
+    if (mount("tmpfs", "/tmp", "tmpfs", MS_NODEV | MS_NOSUID | MS_RELATIME,
+              options) < 0) {
+        perror("mount(/tmp)");
+        return -1;
+    }
+    return 0;
+}
+#endif
+
 static int mount_filesystems()
 {
 #if __linux__
@@ -614,6 +666,12 @@ static int mount_filesystems()
         return -1;
     }
 
+#ifndef TDX
+    if (mount_tmp() < 0) {
+        return -1;
+    }
+#endif
+
     /* May fail if already exists and that's fine. */
     symlink("/proc/self/fd", "/dev/fd");
 #endif
--
